# FFI Bridge Guidelines

Applies to all `extern "C"` Rust code under `crates/*/src/ffi.rs` and the
`crates/ffi_bridge/` crate. See [TODO.md](../../docs/status-docs/TODO.md) Phase 2.

## Purpose

The FFI bridge exposes Rust core crates to the Qt C++ frontend via a stable C ABI.
A single `libseamly_core.dll` / `libseamly_core.so` (the `ffi_bridge` crate) re-exports
all symbols so Qt links against one library.

## Rust FFI File Conventions

- Each crate that exports C symbols has a `src/ffi.rs` module
- `ffi.rs` is declared in `lib.rs` as `pub mod ffi;`
- All exported functions use `#[no_mangle] pub unsafe extern "C"`
- All exported structs that cross the boundary use `#[repr(C)]`

### File Header

```rust
// project: SeamlyLayout
// author: slspencer, copyright 2026
// MIT License: https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT
/// FFI bridge for the <crate_name> crate.
/// Exposes a stable C ABI for use from the Qt 6.11 frontend.
```

## Opaque Handle Pattern

Use opaque `*mut T` handles for Rust structs that Qt should not inspect.
Qt holds the pointer; Rust owns the allocation.

```rust
/// @brief Creates a new SvgDocument from an XML string.
/// @param xml_cstr Null-terminated UTF-8 XML string. Must not be null.
/// @return Opaque pointer to SvgDocument, or null on parse error.
///         Caller must free with svg_dom_free().
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn svg_dom_parse(xml_cstr: *const c_char) -> *mut Document {
    // xml_cstr must not be null
    if xml_cstr.is_null() {
        return std::ptr::null_mut();
    } // if xml_cstr.is_null()
    let xml = match CStr::from_ptr(xml_cstr).to_str() {
        Ok(s) => s,
        Err(_) => return std::ptr::null_mut(),
    };
    match Document::parse(xml) {
        Ok(doc) => Box::into_raw(Box::new(doc)),
        Err(_) => std::ptr::null_mut(),
    } // match Document::parse
}

/// @brief Frees an SvgDocument created by svg_dom_parse().
/// @param doc Opaque pointer returned by svg_dom_parse(). May be null (no-op).
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn svg_dom_free(doc: *mut Document) {
    // doc may be null — no-op in that case
    if !doc.is_null() {
        drop(Box::from_raw(doc));
    } // if !doc.is_null()
}
```

## String Ownership Rules

| Direction | Rule |
|-----------|------|
| Qt → Rust | Qt passes `const char*`; Rust copies immediately via `CStr::to_str()` or `CString::from_raw` — never hold the pointer |
| Rust → Qt | Rust allocates a `CString`, returns `*mut c_char`; Qt **must** call the matching `_free` function |
| Qt must never | Call `free()` or `delete` on a Rust-allocated string — always use the Rust-provided `*_string_free()` |

```rust
/// @brief Serializes an SvgDocument to a null-terminated UTF-8 string.
/// @param doc Opaque pointer. Must not be null.
/// @return Heap-allocated C string. Caller must free with seamly_string_free().
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn svg_dom_to_string(doc: *const Document) -> *mut c_char {
    // doc must not be null
    assert!(!doc.is_null());
    let s = (*doc).to_string();
    CString::new(s).unwrap_or_default().into_raw()
}

/// @brief Frees a string returned by any seamly_* function.
/// @param s Pointer returned by a seamly_* string function. May be null (no-op).
#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn seamly_string_free(s: *mut c_char) {
    // s may be null — no-op in that case
    if !s.is_null() {
        drop(CString::from_raw(s));
    } // if !s.is_null()
}
```

## Error Return Conventions

| Return type | Success | Failure |
|-------------|---------|---------|
| `*mut T` (opaque handle) | Non-null pointer | `null` |
| `c_int` (status code) | `0` | Non-zero (see error codes below) |
| `*mut c_char` (string) | Non-null C string | `null` |

### Error Codes

```c
#define SEAMLY_OK            0
#define SEAMLY_ERR_NULL_ARG  1   /* null pointer passed where not allowed */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_PARSE     2   /* XML / SVG parse error */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_IO        3   /* file read/write error */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_PACK      4   /* layout bin-packing failed (pieces too large) */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_CONVERT   5   /* SVG → DXF conversion error */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_EXPORT    6   /* DXF ASTM export error */
#define SEAMLY_ERR_INVALID   7   /* invalid argument value */
```

Define these in the generated `crates/ffi_bridge/include/seamly_core.h`.

## Progress Callback Convention

For long-running operations (layout, DXF export), accept a C function pointer:

```rust
/// Progress callback type. Called with percent 0–100.
/// @param percent Current progress (0–100).
/// @param user_data Opaque pointer passed through unchanged. May be null.
pub type ProgressFn = unsafe extern "C" fn(percent: c_int, user_data: *mut c_void);

#[no_mangle]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn dxfastm_export(
    drawing: *const Drawing,
    options_json: *const c_char,
    out_path: *const c_char,
    progress_cb: Option<ProgressFn>,  // may be null
    user_data: *mut c_void,
) -> c_int {
    // ...
}
```

On the Qt side, pass a static C function or a lambda-wrapped thunk that posts
progress to `AppController` via `QMetaObject::invokeMethod(..., Qt::QueuedConnection)`.

## cbindgen Configuration

Each FFI crate uses `cbindgen` to auto-generate its C header. Place config at
`crates/<crate>/cbindgen.toml`:

```toml
language = "C"
include_guard = "SEAMLY_<CRATE>_H"
autogen_warning = "/* DO NOT EDIT — generated by cbindgen */"
documentation_style = "doxy"
```

Run via `build.rs`:

```rust
fn main() {
    let crate_dir = std::env::var("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR").unwrap();
    cbindgen::Builder::new()
        .with_crate(crate_dir)
        .with_config(cbindgen::Config::from_file("cbindgen.toml").unwrap())
        .generate()
        .expect("cbindgen failed")
        .write_to_file("include/<crate_name>.h");
}
```

## Qt C++ Usage Pattern

```cpp
#include "seamly_core.h"  // generated by cbindgen

// Load SVG
SvgDocument* doc = svg_dom_parse(xmlUtf8.constData());
if (!doc) {
    emit errorOccurred("Failed to parse SVG");
    return;
}

// Use doc...
char* svgStr = svg_dom_to_string(doc);
QString result = QString::fromUtf8(svgStr);
seamly_string_free(svgStr);   // always free Rust-allocated strings
svg_dom_free(doc);            // always free Rust-allocated handles
```

## Do Not

- Call `delete` or `free()` on any pointer returned by a `seamly_*` function
- Hold a `*const c_char` from Qt across an `await` / thread boundary — copy it first
- Pass Rust-internal pointers (e.g., `&self`) as `user_data` across thread boundaries without `Arc`/`Mutex`
- Expose Rust enum variants directly across FFI — use `c_int` error codes instead
- Return Rust `Result`, `Option`, `String`, `Vec` across FFI — wrap in C-compatible types

## Always

- Null-check every incoming pointer at the top of every FFI function
- Document ownership in Doxygen `@param` / `@return` comments on every FFI function
- Pair every allocation with a matching `_free` function
- Test each FFI function from a C harness in `crates/<crate>/tests/ffi_test.c` before wiring into Qt
